<article class="env-lemma" id="0646">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0646">Lemma <span data-tag="0646">13.19.3</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{A}$ be an abelian category. Assume $\mathcal{A}$ has enough projectives. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
Any object of $\mathcal{A}$ has a projective resolution. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $H^ n(K^\bullet ) = 0$ for all $n \gg 0$ then $K^\bullet $ has a projective resolution. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $K^\bullet $ is a complex with $K^ n = 0$ for $n > a$, then there exists a projective resolution $\alpha : P^\bullet \to K^\bullet $ with $P^ n = 0$ for $n > a$ such that each $\alpha ^ n : P^ n \to K^ n$ is surjective. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Dual to Lemma <a href="/tag/013K" data-tag="013K">13.18.3</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>